{
  "concept": "The fluid force on a submerged vertical surface is built by slicing the surface into thin horizontal strips of nearly constant depth, approximating each strip's force as ρ·h(x*ₖ)·w(x*ₖ)·Δxₖ, and letting the strip count grow so the Riemann sum converges to F = ∫ₐᵇ ρ h(x) w(x) dx.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Downward-pointing vertical depth axis on the left of each panel, arrowhead at bottom, tick marks with labels",
    "Wavy black line across the top of each panel representing the fluid surface",
    "Light-blue vertical gradient fill (pale near surface, deeper blue below) for the fluid region",
    "Irregular closed 'blob' region filled pale orange with a heavy black outline, representing the submerged vertical plate; same silhouette reused in all panels",
    "Panel (a): red dashed horizontal lines at depths a and b touching the top and bottom of the blob, with labels a and b on the axis",
    "Panel (a): label x on the axis at an intermediate depth with a short tick",
    "Panel (a): horizontal blue chord across the blob at depth x with solid black endpoint dots, labeled w(x) above it",
    "Panel (a): double-headed vertical arrow at the right from the wavy surface down to the level of x, labeled h(x)",
    "Panel (b): axis labels a = x₀, x₁, x₂, x₃, vertical dots, x_{n-1}, b = xₙ",
    "Panel (b): red dashed lines from each partition label to the blob, continuing as thin blue horizontal chords across the blob",
    "Panel (b): strip labels A₁, A₂, A₃, vertical ellipsis dots, Aₙ placed inside the corresponding bands",
    "Panel (c): axis labels a, x_{k-1}, x*ₖ (with tick dot), xₖ, b",
    "Panel (c): red dashed line at x*ₖ crossing the blob with black endpoint dots",
    "Panel (c): green-tinted rectangle spanning the blob width between x_{k-1} and xₖ, outlined in black",
    "Panel (c): small vertical double arrow at the right edge of the rectangle labeled Δxₖ",
    "Panel (c): horizontal double-headed arrow with end ticks below the blob labeled w(x*ₖ)",
    "Panel (c): vertical double arrow at far right from the wavy surface to the x*ₖ level labeled h(x*ₖ)",
    "Panel captions (a), (b), (c) centered beneath each panel",
    "Readout box (added for the demo) showing ρ, n, current strip values h(x*ₖ), w(x*ₖ), Δxₖ, the strip force ρh w Δx, the running Riemann sum, and the exact integral value"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "panelView",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "View",
      "options": ["All three panels", "Single animated panel"],
      "default": "All three panels",
      "teaches": "That panels (a), (b), (c) are three stages of one continuous argument — the exact surface, its partition, and the zoomed-in kth strip — rather than three separate pictures."
    },
    {
      "id": "numStrips",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Number of strips n",
      "range": [2, 60, 1],
      "default": 6,
      "teaches": "That refining the partition makes each strip's depth nearly constant, so the rectangle approximation error shrinks and the Riemann sum converges to the exact fluid force integral."
    },
    {
      "id": "stripIndex",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Strip index k",
      "range": [1, 60, 1],
      "default": 3,
      "teaches": "That each strip contributes ρ·h(x*ₖ)·w(x*ₖ)·Δxₖ, and that deeper strips contribute more force per unit area while wider strips contribute more area — the two factors h and w vary independently with depth."
    },
    {
      "id": "accumulate",
      "type": "animation",
      "label": "Accumulate strip forces k = 1 → n",
      "loop": false,
      "teaches": "That the total force is literally the sum of the individual strip forces piling up from the top of the plate to the bottom, with the deep strips dominating the total."
    },
    {
      "id": "inspectPart",
      "type": "hover",
      "teaches": "Which geometric quantity each symbol (a, b, x, h(x), w(x), x*ₖ, Δxₖ, Aₖ) names on the figure, without cluttering the diagram with all definitions at once."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The submerged plate",
      "narration": "A flat plate hangs vertically in fluid, spanning depths from a at the top to b at the bottom. At any depth x the plate has width w(x) and lies h(x) below the surface — the two quantities that control the push of the fluid.",
      "state": { "panelView": "All three panels", "numStrips": 6, "stripIndex": 3 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Why one formula won't do",
      "narration": "Pressure grows with depth, so the force is not simply pressure × area: every level of the plate feels a different pressure. Panel (b) shows the fix — cut the plate into thin horizontal strips A₁…Aₙ along the depth axis.",
      "state": { "panelView": "All three panels", "numStrips": 6, "stripIndex": 1 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Zoom in on one strip",
      "narration": "Slide k to walk down the plate. Panel (c) shows strip k as a rectangle of width w(x*ₖ) and thickness Δxₖ sitting at depth h(x*ₖ), so its force is approximately ρ·h(x*ₖ)·w(x*ₖ)·Δxₖ.",
      "state": { "panelView": "All three panels", "numStrips": 6, "stripIndex": 4 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Add up the strips",
      "narration": "Sweep k from 1 to n and watch the strip forces accumulate into the Riemann sum Σ ρ h(x*ₖ) w(x*ₖ) Δxₖ. Notice how the deepest strips add the largest chunks.",
      "state": { "panelView": "Single animated panel", "numStrips": 6, "stripIndex": 6 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Refine the partition",
      "narration": "Increase n. Each strip's depth becomes nearly constant, the rectangles hug the blob's outline, and the running sum settles onto the exact value of ∫ₐᵇ ρ h(x) w(x) dx.",
      "state": { "panelView": "Single animated panel", "numStrips": 40, "stripIndex": 20 }
    },
    {
      "title": "The limit is the integral",
      "narration": "As n → ∞ the Riemann sum becomes the definite integral F = ∫ₐᵇ ρ h(x) w(x) dx — the fluid-force formula, read directly off the picture.",
      "state": { "panelView": "Single animated panel", "numStrips": 60, "stripIndex": 60 }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Pressure varies with depth, so you can only multiply pressure by area on a strip thin enough that its depth is essentially constant — summing those strip forces and shrinking their thickness is exactly what turns ρ·h·w·Δx into the integral ∫ₐᵇ ρ h(x) w(x) dx.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Three stacked panels, each a rectangular window of fluid: pale-blue vertical gradient (near-white at the top, medium blue at the bottom) with a black wavy line across the very top as the fluid surface. A thin black vertical depth axis runs down the left edge of each panel with a solid arrowhead at the bottom, indicating x increases downward; tick marks with italic serif math labels sit to the left of it. The submerged plate is one irregular closed blob — wide and rounded on top, with a concave notch on its lower-right underside — filled pale orange/peach with a thick black outline, and the identical silhouette is reused in all three panels. Panel (a): red dashed horizontal segments at a (touching the blob's top) and b (touching its bottom); a labeled tick at x mid-blob; a thin blue horizontal chord across the blob at depth x with fat black dots at its two ends, labeled w(x) just above; at the right, a slim double-headed vertical arrow from the wavy surface down to the level of x, labeled h(x). Panel (b): partition labels a = x₀, x₁, x₂, x₃, a vertical ellipsis, x_{n-1}, b = xₙ down the axis; from each, a red dashed segment runs right until it meets the blob, then continues as a thin blue chord across it; bands between chords carry labels A₁, A₂, A₃, a vertical ellipsis of dots, and Aₙ near the bottom. Panel (c): axis labels a, x_{k-1}, x*ₖ (with a dot on the axis), xₖ, b; a red dashed line at x*ₖ crosses the blob with black endpoint dots; a green-tinted rectangle spans the blob's full width between x_{k-1} and xₖ, black-outlined; a small vertical double arrow at its right edge labels Δxₖ; a horizontal double-headed arrow with vertical end ticks sits below the blob labeled w(x*ₖ); a tall thin double arrow at the far right runs from the surface to x*ₖ labeled h(x*ₖ). Italic serif captions (a), (b), (c) are centered under their panels. For the demo, add a compact readout panel (neutral background, monospace numbers) listing ρ, n, k, h(x*ₖ), w(x*ₖ), Δxₖ, the current strip force, the running Riemann sum, and the exact integral, plus a small horizontal convergence bar comparing sum to exact value. Highlight the active strip in saturated green with a darker outline while the other strips stay pale blue chords; strips already accumulated during the animation shade progressively darker orange to visualize the running total. In single-panel mode, merge everything into one large fluid window: the blob with n strips, the active strip called out with the w and h annotation arrows from panel (c)."
}

